  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Late summer surge of heat and humidity brings \nmulti-day severe thunderstorm and excessive rainfall threat to \nkickoff September\n\nA series of mid level impulses tracking eastward along the \nnorthern periphery of an expansive 500mb ridge will continue to \ninteract with anomalous moisture/instability and strong WNW flow\naloft to promote severe storm and excessive rain risk today and\ntomorrow.\n\nThe first cluster of storms is already poised to move off of\nLake Erie into our NW zones between 7 and 8 AM. This cluster has\nnear continuous lightning and was severe warned along the lake\nshore by the Cleveland WFO. Inland from the lake shore, the sfc\nboundary layer is still rather stable this morning with near \n150 J/kg of SBCIN, so this will at least somewhat offset the \nsevere wind potential across our NW zones. However the wind \nthreat could increase to the south and east IF the cluster is \nmaintained until mid-morning, by which time morning sunshine \nwill have eroded the sfc stable layer. Climatologically, \nhowever, these morning MCSs rarely survive the nocturnal to \ndiurnal transition, and several CAMs do show it falling apart \nbefore the boundary layer destabilizes.\n\nThe mesoscale severe weather setup will be complex this\nafternoon into this evening, with key players being the presence\nof a north-south stationary front / backdoor cold front across\nthe eastern zones, and whatever remains of the cold pool from\nthe morning MCS. These boundaries will likely be the focus for\nnew convection initiation this afternoon. Forecast vertical soundings\ndepict large, clockwise turning hodographs associated with a \nstrongly veered wind profile, with 0-3 km SRH on the order of \n200-350 m2/s2 and 0-3km wind shear approaching 40 kts. Combined\nwith MLCAPE on the order of 1500-3000 J/kg, this setup will\nlikely yield both supercells and bowing line segments. If \nsupercells or southeast-bulging bow echos remain on the unstable\nside of the stationary boundary and ingest this highly sheared \ninflow, a couple stronger tornadoes and large hailers can not \nbe ruled out this afternoon into this evening. There will be a\nlarge CAPE gradient across the stationary front, with limited\nsevere potential to its east.\n\nIncreased southwest to westerly flow on Thursday will push the\nbackdoor cold front out of the region, ushering in higher\ntemperatures and dewpoints. The Heat Index will make another run\nat 100-104F across the southern zones, similar to what we saw on\nTuesday. We issued a Heat Advisory valid 11a-8p Thu for this\nlikely threat.\n\nThe severe risk will be focused more across the southern tier of\ncentral PA by Thursday, and south of the PA-MD border on Friday.\nAn upper trough passage suggests additional showers and storms\non Friday, but fortunately the Labor Day weekend is trending \ndrier. The upper ridge is forecast to retrograde by the end of \nthe week which allows for a return to more seasonal temperatures.",
